<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<!-- Camera + photo library. iOS TERMINATES the app on first access if these are absent — no
	     dialog, no catchable error. Required even though capture goes through an HTML
	     <input type="file" capture>, because WKWebView invokes the same system pickers.
	     Copy is specific about what is accessed and why; App Review rejects vague strings. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>B4C uses the camera so you can attach photos of equipment and gear to your department's records.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>B4C uses your photo library so you can attach existing photos to equipment and gear records.</string>
	<!-- Location. Without the when-in-use key WKWebView's geolocation call never invokes EITHER
	     callback, so the clock-in promise never settles and the button spins forever.

	     THE OLD COMMENT HERE SAID "check-in is foreground-only and nothing tracks in the
	     background". That stops being true with the Always key below: automatic station
	     presence monitors a geofence while the app is closed. The claim is corrected rather
	     than left in place, because a stale reassurance in the one file that documents what
	     the app may do is worse than no comment at all.

	     PENDING LEGAL GLANCE — both strings below are member-facing. They are what iOS shows
	     at the permission moment, and unlike the in-app disclosure they cannot be revised
	     without shipping a new build. Counsel cleared the disclosure SCREEN; these two strings
	     were not part of that review. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>B4C confirms you're at your department's approved locations when you check in.</string>
	<!-- The Always string is the one that matters most: iOS shows it when escalating to
	     "Allow all the time", which is the moment a member decides whether to be located while
	     the app is closed. Mirrors the approved in-app disclosure — background collection,
	     purpose, and the two explicit limits (no route tracking, never sold). -->
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>B4C records arrivals and departures at your department's approved locations, even when the app is closed, so your station and duty hours are logged automatically. It does not track your routes and your location is never sold.</string>

	<!-- NSMotionUsageDescription. THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY THE KEY WAS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and
	     that reasoning was sound right up until App Store Connect rejected the upload:

	         ITMS-90683: Missing purpose string in Info.plist (NSMotionUsageDescription)

	     Apple's check is STATIC — it reads the linked binary, not the runtime configuration.
	     TSLocationManager.xcframework links CoreMotion, so the string is required whether or not
	     we ever call into it. There is no build setting that opts out; the only alternatives are
	     to supply the string or to drop the geolocation SDK.

	     WHAT IS STILL TRUE: we run with disableMotionActivityUpdates:true (src/geofence.js), so
	     CMMotionActivityManager is never queried and iOS raises no motion prompt at runtime. The
	     Android side still strips ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION for the same reason — a member who agreed
	     the app may notice they are at the station has not agreed to report what their body is
	     doing. Adding this key does not change that; it satisfies a packaging check.

	     THE STRING SAYS SO PLAINLY rather than inventing a motion feature to justify the key.
	     An earlier draft claimed B4C used motion to detect arrivals; it does not, and a
	     member-facing permission string that overstates collection is the kind of thing that has
	     to match the App Privacy answers on the listing. This one states the truth: the framework
	     includes the APIs, the app does not use them.

	     THE HAZARD IS NOW INVERTED, so read this before changing the geofence config. While
	     disableMotionActivityUpdates stays true the string is accurate. Set it FALSE and the
	     string becomes a false statement to the member — it must be rewritten in the same change,
	     and the App Privacy answers updated with it. -->
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>B4C's location features are built on a framework that includes Apple's motion APIs. B4C does not collect or use motion data — station arrivals and departures are detected from location alone.</string>

	<!-- Background execution. "location" ONLY.
	     iOS wakes a terminated app for a region transition through CLLocationManager, which is
	     what this mode covers and what geofence-only monitoring actually depends on.

	     NOT ADDED, on purpose: "fetch" and "processing", which the vendor's general setup guide
	     lists, serve the SDK's BackgroundFetch/BGTaskScheduler integration — its scheduler and
	     heartbeat features. We use neither; we register one geofence and respond to its
	     transitions. "audio" is listed there only to keep debug sound effects alive, and
	     debug is false.

	     HONEST LIMIT: the vendor's docs do NOT distinguish geofence-only from continuous
	     tracking, so this is reasoned from what the modes are for rather than quoted from a
	     line that says "geofence-only needs exactly this". A compile cannot test it either.
	     If TSBackgroundFetch complains at init on a real device, "processing" (and possibly
	     "fetch") is the first thing to add. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
		<!-- fetch + processing are REQUIRED, and their absence is why iOS compiled but never
		     registered a fence. TSBackgroundFetch ships as a hard dependency of the geolocation
		     SDK and registers BGTaskScheduler work at launch: the binary uses BGAppRefreshTask
		     (which needs "fetch") and BGProcessingTask (which needs "processing"). On iOS 13+,
		     registering a task identifier that is not permitted THROWS during startup — before
		     anything of ours could add a geofence. Verified by reading the shipped framework,
		     not from the setup docs, which describe the Xcode capability checkboxes rather than
		     these plist values. -->
		<string>fetch</string>
		<string>processing</string>
	</array>
	<!-- The identifier TSBackgroundFetch actually registers. Exactly one, read out of the
	     framework binary: com.transistorsoft.fetch. Some setup notes also mention a
	     "customtask" identifier — this version registers no such thing, and listing an
	     identifier nothing registers is harmless but misleading. -->
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.transistorsoft.fetch</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Transistorsoft licence, iOS. Key name VERIFIED, not assumed: TSLocationManagerLicense
	     appears as a string constant in TSLocationManager.xcframework alongside
	     objectForInfoDictionaryKey — the binary this build actually links — and the vendor's
	     setup docs name the same key. Same check that was run for the Android meta-data name.

	     Not a secret: bound to the bundle id, ships inside every build, worthless elsewhere.
	     Order 16956, os=ios, app_id=com.bigbulltech.b4c, entitlement "core".

	     RENEWAL: max_build_stamp 20270828, grace_builds 0 — same date as the Android key. A
	     build made after 28 Aug 2027 will not validate; builds made before it keep working. -->
	<key>TSLocationManagerLicense</key>
	<string>eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSIsImtpZCI6ImVkMjU1MTktbWFpbi12MSJ9..fdbbKKjjBC7E4YAO7IHpDnIdDOUMj8l7OPhPute0qKTqGH7Ks68CQ_wgItUhK3n0Z9Urdh9Y8i_dp5Fcb6aDBQ</string>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>B4C — Before the Call</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
    <false/>
    <key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
    <dict>
      <key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
      <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
            <string>Default Configuration</string>
            <key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
            <string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
            <key>UISceneStoryboardFile</key>
            <string>Main</string>
        </dict>
      </array>
    </dict>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>armv7</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
